A||N||8||Heisenhund||A/C Question||||5||
Z||000000||Heisenhund||04-02-2001||11:11 PM||||Hiya folks,<P>Mulling over the R12 to R134 swap. Aside from being more environmentally friendly (which the rest of the car IS NOT!), and the readily available refills, are there any other advantages to R134? New accumulator, professional pump down & refill of R134 works out to about US$150. Depending on how many pounds of R12 are required (@US$55/pound) the going rate would be about $90. Unless anyone can tell me how many pounds of R12 it takes to fill an otherwise empty system?<P>Thanks,<BR>Heis<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||04-02-2001||11:25 PM||||The capacity is written on a green sticker on the evap unit . I think it's 2 1/2 lbs.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Heisenhund||04-02-2001||11:34 PM||||OK, that just upped the price a little. Now it's about an even trade, financially speaking.<P>Heis<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula88||04-02-2001||11:41 PM||||Well, R134a is not as efficient as R12, but the price of R12 will only rise as it's produced less and less and taxed more and more.  Back in the '80's before they knew R12 would damage the atmosphere, it sold for about $1 a pound.  The extra price now is mainly due to regulation and taxation.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||04-04-2001||07:55 AM||||I've converted my 89 Bonneville (4 years ago), my 85SE v-6, my daughter's 88 Camry, and my other daughter's 90 Protege.  No problems and no noticable difference in performance.  Much cheaper to maintain if a leak developes.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||04-04-2001||08:13 AM||||I converted my son's 88GT to R134A- cools fine.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Oreif||04-04-2001||09:42 AM||||Our club had a meeting with an A/C guy who has done lots of cars, including r134a conversions. His thing was that there will always be r12 around. Many places still use it for building A/C units. Yes it is expensive but he said it isn't going anywhere. As for conversions, He said if the r12 system is working stay with r12. (if it ain't broke don't fix it) But if you need major component replacement that is the time to switch to r134a. He also stressed NOT to use the "kits" sold in autoparts stores. Something about the chemicals (ester?)in the oil used in the kits isn't too good for GM type A/C units. He suggested to have a shop do it using the correct stuff.||||reg||1
Z||000007||DRH||04-04-2001||01:23 PM||||I'm still kicking myself in the butt for not buying a case or two of the stuff when it was still around a buck a can in the late 80s. I work for an AC company so I was more aware of the environmental issues and what was coming than most people. I KNEW the price was going to get ridiculous. I even though about doing it, but I never got around to it...<P>$60 x 24 = $1440... vs. the $25 - $30 to buy it then.<P>Of course they'll put you under the jail for selling it, but I could have had my own personal lifetime supply. <P>My 88 still cools fine. When it doesn't anymore, I plan to bite the bullet and go with R134...||||reg||9
Z||000008||theogre||04-04-2001||07:18 PM||||The charge lable can be hard to find. it's up front but is often hiding behind a heater hose.<P>Keep in mind that Fiero has about the same AC system as cars much bigger in size. A small loss of effiency is not likely to be noticed except in the hottest climates.<P>There are notes in my cave on this.<P>One of these days when I'm not broke I'll replace my compressor and put in R134.||||reg||1
